From 512a02064901a280f8781c5f6aeafa519360e29f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: autoresearch <agent@localhost>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 07:19:17 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/5] ggml: do not split skinny matmuls across threads

The x-asr encoder's GEMMs run at 3-24 columns (a 24-frame chunk after [1,2,4,8,4,2] time downsampling) and it
issues ~65k of them per 69 s clip, so every call was paying a two-thread dispatch and a barrier to hand one or
two columns to the second core. The phone's ASR-only run averages 0.98 cores on a 2-cpu mask, which is what that
looks like. Below 16 columns the rows now stay on one thread.

Which core runs a row, and in what order, is unchanged, so this is bit-identical - and it is the same work-size
idea already applied to the copy/elementwise group, where it measured 0% precisely because those ops sit beside
the matmuls rather than on the critical path. Here they ARE the critical path: MUL_MAT is 52% of the ASR leg.
---
 src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c | 12 +++++++++++-
 1 file changed, 11 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c b/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
index 14c06a9..59a1804 100644
--- a/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
+++ b/src/ggml-cpu/ggml-cpu.c
@@ -2403,7 +2403,17 @@ static int ggml_get_n_tasks(struct ggml_tensor * node, int n_threads) {
         case GGML_OP_MUL_MAT_ID:
         case GGML_OP_OUT_PROD:
             {
-                n_tasks = n_threads;
+                // Splitting a matmul across threads only pays when there are enough columns to divide. The
+                // x-asr encoder runs its GEMMs at 3-24 columns (a 24-frame chunk after [1,2,4,8,4,2] time
+                // downsampling), and it issues ~65k of them per 69 s clip - so each call was paying a
+                // two-thread dispatch and a barrier to hand one or two columns to the second core. Measured
+                // on the phone, the ASR-only run averages 0.98 cores on a 2-cpu mask, which is what that looks
+                // like. Below the threshold the rows stay on one thread. Which core runs a row, and in what
+                // order, is unchanged, so this is bit-identical - and the same work-size idea already applied
+                // to the copy/elementwise group, where it measured 0% because those ops sit beside the matmuls
+                // rather than on the critical path.
+                const int64_t cols = node->ne[1];
+                n_tasks = cols >= 16 ? n_threads : 1;
             } break;
         case GGML_OP_GET_ROWS:
         case GGML_OP_SET_ROWS:
